    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.is_branching - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.is_branching.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_branching()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if the graph is a branching (a directed forest).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_branching()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if the graph is a branching (a directed forest).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.is_branching()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Check if the graph is a branching (a directed forest).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCheck if the graph is a branching (a directed forest).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L90809-L90902){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.is_branching()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Is branching](../assets/graphs/is-branching.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a directed forest of two trees, A pointing to B and C, and D pointing to E; every node has at most one incoming edge, so is_branching() returns True. Right: the same graph plus an edge from D to C (shown in the accent color); C now has two parents, A and D, so is_branching() returns False.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA branching is a directed graph with no cycles, even when edge directions are ignored,\nin which every node has at most one incoming edge. Each of its weakly connected\ncomponents is then an arborescence: a tree hanging from a root with all edges pointing\naway from it. A branching that is a single tree is an arborescence (see\n`is_arborescence()`), just as an undirected forest made of one tree is a tree.\n\nBranchings are what `maximum_branching()` and `minimum_branching()` return, and they\nmodel any \"each item has at most one parent\" structure: org charts with several heads,\nfile systems with several roots, or the infection trees of separate outbreaks.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   `True` if the graph is a branching. Undirected and empty graphs return `False`.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe check makes a single pass over the edges, counting in-degrees and merging the\nendpoints in a union-find structure: a second incoming edge, or an edge whose ends are\nalready connected (which includes self-loops and parallel edges), rules out a\nbranching. It runs in nearly $O(V + E)$ time.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_arborescence`) \n\n:   Whether the graph is a single directed tree.\n\n[](`~is_forest`) \n\n:   The undirected version of a branching.\n\n[](`~get_in_degree`) \n\n:   Count a node's incoming edges.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nTwo separate trees, each rooted at its own node, form a branching:\n\n::: {#be5f71da .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ng = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"D\", \"E\")], directed=True\n)\n\ng.is_branching(), g.is_arborescence()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(True, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nGiving C a second parent breaks the \"at most one incoming edge\" rule:\n\n::: {#d24551cc .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ng.add_edge(\"D\", \"C\")\ng.is_branching()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\nFalse\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Four departments\n\nIn a company's reporting lines, each edge runs from a manager to someone who reports to\nthem. Without the chief executive, Mira, the four department heads have no manager, so the\nchart is four arborescences: a branching, but no longer an arborescence. A second manager\nfor anyone, such as Owen also reporting to Hiro, would break the branching too:\n\n::: {#be3ce677 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\norg = ec.org_chart().delete_node(\"Mira\")\nheads = [\n    org.get_label_from_id(n)\n    for n in org.get_node_ids()\n    if org.get_in_degree(n) == 0\n]\n\norg.is_branching(), org.is_arborescence(), sorted(heads)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(True, False, ['Jonas', 'Leila', 'Sam', 'Uma'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe four department heads, the roots of the branching, are highlighted:\n\n::: {#b10b0fe2 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\norg.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", highlight=heads, label_by=\"label\", width=820, height=640\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#94949